{"schema_version":3,"catalogue_version":1,"id":"bco:17","provision":"BCO 17","book":"bco","book_name":"Book of Church Order","abbreviation":"BCO","reference":"17","title":"Chapter 17 · Doctrine of Ordination","supplementary":false,"canonical_url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/provisions/bco/17/","reader_url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-constitution-reader/#bco/17","current_text":{"html":"<section class=\"provision-chapter-section\" id=\"bco-17-1\"><h3><a href=\"/pca-ga/provisions/bco/17-1/\">BCO 17-1</a></h3><div class=\"provision-text\"><p>Those who have been called to office in the Church are to be inducted by the ordination of a court.</p></div></section><section class=\"provision-chapter-section\" id=\"bco-17-2\"><h3><a href=\"/pca-ga/provisions/bco/17-2/\">BCO 17-2</a></h3><div class=\"provision-text\"><p>Ordination is the authoritative admission of one duly called to an office in the Church of God, accompanied with prayer and the laying on of hands, to which it is proper to add the giving of the right hand of fellowship.</p></div></section><section class=\"provision-chapter-section\" id=\"bco-17-3\"><h3><a href=\"/pca-ga/provisions/bco/17-3/\">BCO 17-3</a></h3><div class=\"provision-text\"><p>As every ecclesiastical office, according to the Scriptures, is a special charge, no man shall be ordained unless it be to the performance of a definite work.</p></div></section>","text":"BCO 17-1Those who have been called to office in the Church are to be inducted by the ordination of a court.BCO 17-2Ordination is the authoritative admission of one duly called to an office in the Church of God, accompanied with prayer and the laying on of hands, to which it is proper to add the giving of the right hand of fellowship.BCO 17-3As every ecclesiastical office, according to the Scriptures, is a special charge, no man shall be ordained unless it be to the performance of a definite work."},"source":{"name":"PCA Constitution Reader","revision":"786a516e34736a